EVEREST hostile review found a second-order replay defect in exact head `a7339936848c4e2ab066902d0ae7812ffc84cad5` before merge.

The new monotonic-time guard executes before duplicate detection:

1. apply `evt_A` at 01:00 -> state advances;
2. apply a different valid event `evt_B` at 02:00 -> `state.updatedAt = 02:00`;
3. replay the original identical `evt_A` at 01:00 after restart/redelivery;
4. `eventAt < state.updatedAt` returns `event-time-regression` before the code looks up `evt_A` in `eventLog`.

That breaks the module's existing idempotency contract for legitimate delayed/redelivered duplicates. A duplicate of an already-recorded event must remain `DUPLICATE_IGNORED` even after later events have advanced the state clock; only a previously unseen event is eligible for the monotonic-time rejection. Same-id/different-content must still surface `event-id-identity-collision` rather than being masked by time ordering.

Required repair before merge: validate the explicit timestamp enough to build deterministic identity, perform durable event-id duplicate/collision resolution first, then apply `event-time-regression` only to new events. Add a hostile regression with `evt_A@01:00 -> evt_B@02:00 -> replay evt_A@01:00` expecting `DUPLICATE_IGNORED`, plus an old same-id/different-content replay expecting `event-id-identity-collision`.

